// SENTINEL Field mobile enrollment ingress (WP-26, architecture sections 61,
// 62, 62.1). Owned exclusively by
// services/core-api/src/modules/device-enrollment-ingress. This file is
// PERSISTENCE ONLY: every rule it serves is already frozen in
// packages/contracts/src/device-identity.ts and device-signature.ts, and every
// registry fact it names is owned by prisma/schema/shield.prisma. Nothing here
// re-states a rule the contracts own or a fact the registry owns; these two
// tables exist so the D26-04A attestation ceremony and the D26-04B provider
// record are DURABLE.
//
// THE INVARIANT THIS SCHEMA EXISTS TO HOLD
// ----------------------------------------
//
//     A BOOTSTRAP GRANT CREATES ZERO DEVICE AUTHORITY.
//
//     issuer != approver != intended user,
//     and a human still approves THE EXACT REQUEST FINGERPRINT.
//
// shield.prisma states the registry's version of this and device-gateway.prisma
// states the gateway's. WP-26 is where a PHYSICAL phone first speaks to the
// server holding a key that is registered nowhere, so it is where the
// temptation to let the ceremony's own state become a credential is strongest.
// Neither table below is a credential. Steal every column in both and you hold
// a freshness value and a record of what a verifier concluded; you hold no
// device identity, no session, and no approval.
//
// THE ATTESTATION CHALLENGE IS NOT A SECRET (D26-04A)
// ---------------------------------------------------
// `challenge_value` is >= 256 bits of SERVER randomness, and it exists for one
// reason: Android Key Attestation is baked in AT KEY GENERATION, so the server
// nonce must exist BEFORE the phone generates its key. A server that does not
// compare the value inside the attestation certificate against its own
// challenge can be handed an old certificate. That makes this column a
// FRESHNESS VALUE, never a credential — it is returned to the phone in the
// clear, it is embedded in a certificate the phone then hands back, and
// possession of it authorises precisely nothing.
//
// THE LIVE / HISTORY SPLIT (the WP-17A rule, applied to WP-26)
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// The doctrine header of prisma/schema/field.prisma states the locked ruling,
// shield.prisma applies it to the registry and device-gateway.prisma applies it
// to the gateway. Tables are divided by what they ASSERT.
//
//   CEREMONY STATE
//     DeviceAttestationChallenge asserts something true *right now* — "this
//     attestation ceremony is in flight, for this tenant, this site, this
//     intended user and this bootstrap grant". So every Site, User and grant it
//     names must exist and must belong to the same organisation. Each reference
//     is COMPOSITE, over the (id, organisation_id) candidate keys
//     `sites_id_organisation_key` (WP-17A), `users_id_organisation_key`
//     (WP-24/D24-04a) and `bootstrap_grant_id_organisation_key` (WP-24), rather
//     than a plain FK to `id`. A plain FK would still admit a row pairing one
//     tenant's organisation with another tenant's site, user or grant; the
//     composite reference makes the DATABASE reject it.
//
//     Every one of them is onDelete: Restrict. A grant's, a site's or a user's
//     lifecycle must never silently delete the record of a challenge Sentinel
//     issued, because a challenge row that vanishes does not become an expired
//     challenge — it becomes a challenge the server can no longer make a
//     statement about, and the one-shot property would be lost with it.
//
//   HISTORICAL ARTEFACT
//     AndroidKeyAttestationArtifact carries NO Site, User, Device, grant or
//     challenge foreign key AT ALL, per the historical-artefact rule the WP-17A
//     header states and which shield.prisma applies to DeviceSecurityEvent,
//     DeviceTrustTransition, DeviceAttestationObservation and
//     DeviceNonceConsumption, and device-gateway.prisma applies to
//     DeviceGatewayOperationEvent. Its identifiers are the identifiers AS
//     RECORDED at the moment the verifier ran. `Cascade` would let a lifecycle
//     erase the record of what the verifier decided — forbidden by section 61.
//     `Restrict` would mean a grant could never be removed once a single chain
//     had ever been evaluated, silently inventing a retirement policy this work
//     package has no mandate to decide. Integrity comes from the write-time
//     check instead: the ingress proves the tenant, the site, the user, the
//     grant and the challenge all agree BEFORE the verifier is invoked.
//
// NOTHING IN THIS FILE EVER CASCADES.
//
// THE RAW CHAIN IS RESTRICTED, AND THIS IS THE ONLY PLACE IT LIVES (D26-04B)
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `DeviceAttestationEvaluationInput` carries an organisation, optional
// identifiers, a public-key thumbprint and a server instant — not a certificate
// chain — and `EnrollmentRequestSubmission` has no attestation blob either.
// That is not a gap to be fixed by stuffing a base64 chain into a frozen
// contract or an audit payload. `certificate_chain_der` below is the ONE column
// in Sentinel that holds the raw evidence, and the rule that governs it is
// absolute:
//
//     the raw chain NEVER appears in an audit payload,
//     NEVER in a request fingerprint as raw bytes,
//     NEVER in a general application log,
//     and NEVER in a client-readable response.
//
// Shield receives only `artifact_id` — an opaque, server-generated reference —
// and the verdict. Everything else on this row is a PARSED SCALAR: an integer
// security level, a boolean device-lock state, a package name. A scalar cannot
// smuggle a blob.
//
// See docs/execution/directives/WP-26-field-mobile-foundation.md for the
// governing directive (D26-01, D26-02, D26-04A, D26-04B, D26-09, D26-11) and
// the WP-23 contracts for every vocabulary this file stores.
//
// A NOTE ON ENUM COLUMNS. The attestation outcome is stored as TEXT, not as a
// Postgres enum type, for the reason shield.prisma, device-gateway.prisma and
// whisper.prisma all state: the vocabulary and its transition table are owned
// by the frozen contracts and versioned in reviewable code, not in a database
// type whose every addition would be a migration. The contract is the
// authority; the column is storage. The Android security levels, key purposes,
// origins and verified-boot states are stored as the INTEGERS the platform
// itself defines, because they are the platform's vocabulary and not Sentinel's
// — translating them into words here would be this file inventing a mapping the
// verifier would then have to agree with.

// ===========================================================================
// CEREMONY STATE
// ===========================================================================

/// The D26-04A pre-key-generation attestation challenge. ONE-SHOT,
/// SHORT-LIVED, AND NOT A SECRET.
///
/// `id` IS the `attestation_challenge_id` — not a surrogate key beside one.
/// There is exactly one identifier for an attestation ceremony and the server
/// owns it.
///
/// WHY THIS TABLE EXISTS AT ALL. Android Key Attestation is produced WHEN THE
/// KEY IS GENERATED: `setAttestationChallenge()` places the relying party's
/// challenge inside the attestation certificate precisely so the key can be
/// shown to have been created in response to a specific request. A sequence in
/// which the phone generates a key and then submits it is replayable, because
/// nothing in the submission distinguishes a certificate minted seconds ago
/// from one minted last year. So the server issues the nonce FIRST, durably,
/// and compares it against the value inside the certificate afterwards.
///
/// THIS IS NOT A SECRET, AND NOTHING MAY EVER TREAT IT AS ONE.
///
/// Steal every column in this table — `id`, `challenge_value`, the site, the
/// intended user, the grant — and you have ZERO device authority. The
/// enrollment still requires the one-shot bootstrap grant secret (which is NOT
/// stored here and is never embedded in a certificate), the intended user's
/// independent authenticated session, a StrongBox key whose private half never
/// leaves the hardware, and an independent commander's approval of the exact
/// request fingerprint.
///
/// LIFETIME. `DEVICE_ATTESTATION_CHALLENGE_MAX_AGE_MS` = 120_000, with an
/// EXCLUSIVE `expires_at` boundary, evaluated at request time — there is no
/// expiry scheduler, per D25-08's still-binding constraint against new
/// background schedulers. The window is additionally CLAMPED so a challenge can
/// never outlive its bootstrap grant: an old attestation is not accepted merely
/// because the grant still has time left, and a grant that is already dead
/// cannot mint a challenge at all.
///
/// CEREMONY STATE — composite tenant-aware relations, Restrict. See this
/// file's header.
model DeviceAttestationChallenge {
  /// The `attestation_challenge_id` itself.
  id String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid

  organisationId String @map("organisation_id") @db.Text
  siteId String @map("site_id") @db.Text
  /// Enrollment provenance, NOT hardware identity (C14-02). The intended user
  /// is ALSO the human whose live session the ingress equality-binds before
  /// this row is written — but this column is the record of the binding, never
  /// the answer to it.
  intendedUserId String @map("intended_user_id") @db.Text
  /// The ceremony this challenge belongs to. A challenge is meaningless
  /// without it: the grant is what a commander authorised, and the challenge is
  /// only the freshness value inside that authorisation.
  bootstrapGrantId String @map("bootstrap_grant_id") @db.Uuid

  /// >= 256 bits of SERVER randomness, canonical unpadded base64url.
  ///
  /// NOT A SECRET — a freshness value. The phone receives it in the clear, and
  /// hands it back embedded in a certificate that anyone holding the device
  /// could read. What it buys is the one property a relying party cannot get
  /// any other way: proof that THIS key was generated in response to THIS
  /// request, and not before it.
  challengeValue String @map("challenge_value") @db.Text

  issuedAt DateTime @map("issued_at") @db.Timestamptz(3)
  /// EXCLUSIVE boundary, per the existing WP-23/WP-24/WP-25 doctrine, and
  /// clamped to the bootstrap grant's own expiry. See this model's header.
  expiresAt DateTime @map("expires_at") @db.Timestamptz(3)

  /// NULL while the challenge stands. Stamped by a fenced conditional update in
  /// the SAME statement that claims it, which is what makes the challenge
  /// one-shot IN FACT and not merely by intention. It is never cleared: a spent
  /// challenge does not become unspent, and a verification that failed does not
  /// hand the value back — the phone discards the unfinished key and restarts
  /// with a new challenge, which is exactly what D26-04A prescribes.
  consumedAt DateTime? @map("consumed_at") @db.Timestamptz(3)

  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // C18-03 — THE DURABLE RECEIPT FOR ONE SUBMISSION.
  //
  // A successful enrollment request must be able to survive a LOST RESPONSE.
  // Shield creates the request, the HTTP response never arrives, the phone
  // retries the identical submission — and before these columns existed it was
  // told `ATTESTATION_CHALLENGE_ALREADY_CONSUMED` and never learned its own
  // request id or fingerprint. That regresses the convergence doctrine Shield
  // itself established for a repeated submission under one grant.
  //
  // Spending a challenge on an ATTEMPT is still right and is unchanged. What is
  // added is the ability to PROVE that a particular consumed challenge already
  // produced a particular enrollment request, so an exact retry can be answered
  // with that request rather than with a refusal.
  //
  // EVERY ONE OF THESE FAILS CLOSED. They are nullable, they are never cleared,
  // and a consumed challenge whose outcome is not fully recorded resolves to
  // NOTHING — the retry refuses. Nothing here un-consumes a challenge, extends
  // an expiry, or authorises a second attempt.
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------

  /// WHICH submission spent this challenge. A digest over the whole material
  /// content of the submission — the grant, this challenge, the public key, the
  /// attestation chain as submitted, and the custody terms — stamped in the SAME
  /// fenced update that stamps `consumed_at`, so it is never possible for the
  /// row to say "consumed" without saying by what.
  ///
  /// A retry whose fingerprint differs in ANY of those is a DIFFERENT ceremony
  /// wearing a spent challenge, and refuses.
  submissionFingerprint String? @map("submission_fingerprint") @db.Text

  /// The enrollment request this submission produced, AS RECORDED. A plain
  /// column with no foreign key, following this file's historical-artefact rule:
  /// the ingress may not couple its own lifecycle to a Shield table, and it may
  /// not read one either (D26-09). What it may do is remember the answer Shield
  /// gave it.
  enrollmentRequestId String? @map("enrollment_request_id") @db.Uuid
  /// The fingerprint the operative must be able to read back — it is the value
  /// the independent commander will be asked to confirm out of band, and a
  /// ceremony where the two humans cannot compare the same value is a ceremony
  /// in which the approval means less.
  enrollmentRequestFingerprint String? @map("enrollment_request_fingerprint") @db.Text
  /// The SERVER's attestation verdict for this submission, echoed on the retry
  /// so the answer is byte-identical rather than merely equivalent.
  attestationOutcome String? @map("attestation_outcome") @db.Text
  /// The key storage the server DERIVED from that verdict (D26-02). Recorded,
  /// never claimed.
  keyStorage String? @map("key_storage") @db.Text

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(3)
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(3)

  /// Composite, over `sites_id_organisation_key`, `users_id_organisation_key`
  /// and `bootstrap_grant_id_organisation_key`: the database itself rejects a
  /// challenge pairing org-A with a site, an intended user or a bootstrap grant
  /// belonging to org-B. Restrict, per this file's header.
  site Site @relation(fields: [siteId, organisationId], references: [id, organisationId], onDelete: Restrict, onUpdate: Cascade)
  intendedUser User @relation("AttestationChallengeIntendedUser", fields: [intendedUserId, organisationId], references: [id, organisationId], onDelete: Restrict, onUpdate: Cascade)
  bootstrapGrant EnrollmentBootstrapGrant @relation(fields: [bootstrapGrantId, organisationId], references: [id, organisationId], onDelete: Restrict, onUpdate: Cascade)

  /// ONE CHALLENGE VALUE PER TENANT. Two live challenges carrying the same
  /// value would be two ceremonies competing over one attestation certificate,
  /// and the server would be unable to say which key was generated for which
  /// request.
  @@unique([organisationId, challengeValue], map: "device_attestation_challenge_value_key")
  /// "What attestation ceremonies are outstanding behind this grant." A grant
  /// may legitimately mint SEVERAL challenges over its life — an expired
  /// challenge means the phone discards the unfinished key and restarts — so
  /// this is an index, deliberately not a uniqueness constraint.
  @@index([organisationId, bootstrapGrantId])
  @@map("device_attestation_challenges")
}

// ===========================================================================
// HISTORICAL ARTEFACT — append-only, and the ONE restricted store
// ===========================================================================

/// ONE ANDROID KEY ATTESTATION EVALUATION, as performed. APPEND-ONLY.
///
/// This is the D26-04B provider record: the server's own account of what it
/// concluded about one certificate chain, at one instant, against one version
/// of its trust material. Shield never sees it. Shield receives `artifact_id` —
/// an opaque server-generated reference that fits the frozen
/// `DeviceAttestationEvidence.attestation_reference` field — plus the outcome,
/// and nothing else. A structure with nowhere to put a blob cannot leak one,
/// and the frozen evidence structure has nowhere to put one.
///
/// NO LIFECYCLE FOREIGN KEY AT ALL — not to Site, User, Device, the bootstrap
/// grant, or even the attestation challenge this row names. That is the WP-17A
/// historical-artefact rule, and the reasoning is unchanged: `Cascade` would let
/// a lifecycle erase the record of what the verifier decided, and `Restrict`
/// would invent a retirement policy this work package has no mandate to decide.
/// The identifiers below are the identifiers AS RECORDED at the moment of
/// evaluation.
///
/// WHY THE VERSIONS ARE COLUMNS. A verdict is only meaningful relative to the
/// trust material it was reached against. `verifier_version`,
/// `trust_anchor_set_version` and `revocation_snapshot_version` are recorded so
/// that a later reviewer can answer "what did the server know when it said
/// that?" without guessing. A verdict whose trust material cannot be named is
/// not evidence; it is an assertion.
///
/// WHAT MAY NEVER LEAVE THIS ROW. `certificate_chain_der` is the raw evidence
/// and it is RESTRICTED: no audit payload, no fingerprint input, no application
/// log, no client-readable response. Every other column here is a parsed scalar
/// precisely so that the useful facts can travel without the blob travelling
/// with them.
model AndroidKeyAttestationArtifact {
  /// The `artifact_id` itself — SERVER GENERATED. There is no parameter
  /// anywhere through which a caller could supply one, for the reason D24-04
  /// gives the sequence namespace and D24-10A gives a proposed key identity: a
  /// value a client can choose is a value a client can shape history with.
  id String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid

  organisationId String @map("organisation_id") @db.Text

  /// AS RECORDED. Plain columns, no foreign key — see this model's header.
  bootstrapGrantId String @map("bootstrap_grant_id") @db.Uuid
  attestationChallengeId String @map("attestation_challenge_id") @db.Uuid

  /// The canonical thumbprint of the key this chain was evaluated FOR (C15-02:
  /// computed from the key, never believed). The evaluator re-binds an incoming
  /// artifact reference against this column and the organisation, so a
  /// reference borrowed from another ceremony resolves to nothing.
  publicKeyThumbprint String @map("public_key_thumbprint") @db.Text
  /// SHA-256, lowercase hex, over the canonical concatenation of the submitted
  /// DER certificates. A HANDLE, not the evidence: it lets two submissions be
  /// compared, and a log or an audit row name a chain, without the bytes.
  certificateChainHash String @map("certificate_chain_hash") @db.Text

  verifierVersion String @map("verifier_version") @db.Text
  trustAnchorSetVersion String @map("trust_anchor_set_version") @db.Text
  revocationSnapshotVersion String @map("revocation_snapshot_version") @db.Text

  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // PARSED SECURITY CLAIMS — structured scalars, every one of them NULLABLE
  //
  // Nullable because a refused evaluation may never have got far enough to read
  // them: an unparseable chain has no security level, and a chain that failed
  // to validate has no claims worth recording as though they had been believed.
  // A NULL here means "not established", never "false".
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------

  /// The KeyDescription's own version number.
  attestationVersion Int? @map("attestation_version")
  /// 0 = Software, 1 = TrustedEnvironment, 2 = StrongBox. The platform's
  /// vocabulary, stored as the platform's integers. D26-03A: a certificate
  /// saying TEE is NEVER promoted into the StrongBox profile.
  attestationSecurityLevel Int? @map("attestation_security_level")
  keymasterSecurityLevel Int? @map("keymaster_security_level")
  /// The teeEnforced authorisation list, read as scalars. `key_purposes` is the
  /// SET as recorded, sorted ascending, so a purpose the verifier refused is
  /// still auditable.
  keyPurposes Int[] @map("key_purposes")
  keyAlgorithm Int? @map("key_algorithm")
  keySize Int? @map("key_size")
  keyEcCurve Int? @map("key_ec_curve")
  keyOrigin Int? @map("key_origin")
  noAuthRequired Boolean? @map("no_auth_required")
  /// RootOfTrust: 0 = Verified, 1 = SelfSigned, 2 = Unverified, 3 = Failed.
  verifiedBootState Int? @map("verified_boot_state")
  deviceLocked Boolean? @map("device_locked")
  /// The AttestationApplicationId, as read from the certificate. It is the
  /// application identity the chain CLAIMS; the verifier compares it against
  /// server configuration and this column records what was compared.
  attestationPackageName String? @map("attestation_package_name")
  /// SHA-256, lowercase hex, of the signing certificate the package was signed
  /// with, as carried by the extension.
  attestationSigningDigest String? @map("attestation_signing_digest")

  /// VERIFIED | NEGATIVE | INVALID | REVOKED | UNAVAILABLE
  /// (`DeviceAttestationOutcomeSchema`). C14-05's split is frozen and this
  /// column stores its result, never a re-interpretation of it.
  outcome String @db.Text
  /// The verifier's OWN precise reason, from its internal vocabulary. It is
  /// deliberately richer than anything a caller is ever told: the external
  /// answer for every refusal is identical, and the reason lives here where an
  /// operator can read it and an attacker cannot.
  outcomeReason String @map("outcome_reason") @db.Text

  /// The AUTHORITATIVE SERVER instant the evaluation was performed at, passed
  /// in from the transaction rather than read from a second clock — the reason
  /// `DeviceAttestationEvaluator.evaluate` takes `now` as an argument.
  evaluatedAt DateTime @map("evaluated_at") @db.Timestamptz(3)

  /// THE RESTRICTED COLUMN. The raw submitted chain, as canonical base64 DER,
  /// one certificate per element, leaf first. See this file's header for the
  /// four places it may never appear.
  certificateChainDer String[] @map("certificate_chain_der")

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(3)

  /// "Show me every evaluation this key was ever the subject of." The
  /// evaluator's re-binding lookup is by primary key and organisation; this
  /// index serves review, not the hot path.
  @@index([organisationId, publicKeyThumbprint])
  /// "Show me what this ceremony's verifier concluded."
  @@index([organisationId, attestationChallengeId])
  @@map("android_key_attestation_artifacts")
}
